@* No <NotFound> fragment: this is a Blazor Web App, and the framework does not use one.

   Precisely, because "removed" and "deprecated" are different claims and only one is true:
   Router.NotFound still EXISTS on .NET 10 and still compiles - the ref assembly carries
   [Obsolete("NotFound is deprecated. Use NotFoundPage instead.")]. What it no longer does is work.
   It was retained inert for back-compat in .NET 8/9, and Microsoft's .NET 10 routing documentation
   states the render fragment "isn't supported in .NET 10 or later". So deleting it also removes an
   obsolete-API use, not just dead markup.

   The markup that used to sit here promised "Sorry, there's nothing at this address." for an unknown
   URL. No reachable caller can trigger it - Router renders the fragment only for non-intercepted
   (programmatic) navigations, and every NavigationManager.NavigateTo in this project targets one of
   the twelve real @page routes. An unmatched path is answered by the ASP.NET Core pipeline instead:
   a bare 404 for a page route, or the JSON problem body in Radio.Web.Configuration.ApiNotFound for
   anything under /api/ (UI-11).

   The supported replacement is the NotFoundPage parameter (NotFoundPage="typeof(Pages.NotFound)").
   Deliberately not adopted: this service's unmatched-path behaviour is a pipeline concern, and a
   friendly HTML 404 for page routes is a separate decision that must exclude /api/. *@
<Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
  <Found Context="routeData">
    <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)" />
    <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
  </Found>
</Router>
